## 5. OSINT — Open-Source Intelligence

Open-Source Intelligence (OSINT) is the process of collecting and analyzing information from publicly available sources.

### 5.1 Google Search Operators

Search operators help narrow search results.

| Operator | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `site:example.com` | Restricts results to a domain |
| `inurl:admin` | Searches for pages with `admin` in the URL |
| `intitle:admin` | Searches for pages with `admin` in the title |
| `filetype:pdf` | Searches for PDF files |
| `intext:password` | Searches for pages containing the specified text |

Example:

```text
site:tryhackme.com filetype:pdf
```

Search results depend on what the search engine has indexed. These operators do not reveal every resource on a website.

Use search techniques within an appropriate, lawful scope. Finding exposed information does not authorize access to it or its use.

### 5.2 Wappalyzer

Wappalyzer helps identify technologies used by a website, potentially including:

- Content management systems (CMSs)
- Application frameworks
- JavaScript libraries
- Analytics tools
- Content delivery networks (CDNs)
- Some software versions

Website: https://www.wappalyzer.com/

Technology detection is based on observable signals and may be incomplete or inaccurate.

### 5.3 Wayback Machine

The Internet Archive's Wayback Machine provides archived snapshots of many websites.

Website: https://web.archive.org/

Archived content can help researchers understand historical website structure, previously published pages, and changes over time.

Not every page or historical version is available. Archived information may also be outdated.

### 5.4 GitHub

Public Git repositories, source files, and commit histories can reveal implementation details.

In some cases, a secret removed from the latest version remains present in an earlier commit.

If a real credential or token is discovered:

- Do not use it to access an account or service.
- Do not publish or redistribute it.
- Report it through the appropriate security contact or disclosure process.

### 5.5 Amazon S3

Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3) is an object-storage service.

Example bucket-name patterns might include:

```text
company-assets
company-backup
company-dev
```

Bucket names and publicly discoverable URLs can provide clues about an organization's infrastructure, but neither proves that the bucket is publicly accessible or vulnerable.

Assess storage permissions only when explicitly authorized. Do not access, download, or modify data outside the approved scope.

