## 6. Gobuster — Automated Content Discovery

Gobuster is a command-line tool that uses wordlists to discover web paths, DNS subdomains, and virtual hosts.

### Wordlists

A common SecLists installation path on Linux is:

```text
/usr/share/wordlists/SecLists/
```

Useful wordlists include:

```text
Discovery/Web-Content/common.txt
Discovery/Web-Content/directory-list-2.3-medium.txt
Discovery/DNS/subdomains-top1million-5000.txt
```

The exact location depends on the operating system and installation method.

### 6.1 `dir` Mode — Directory and File Discovery

Example:

```bash
gobuster dir \
  -u "http://TARGET-IP" \
  -w /usr/share/wordlists/SecLists/Discovery/Web-Content/common.txt
```

This mode tests candidate paths against the target web server.

#### Common Options

| Option | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `-u` | Specifies the target URL |
| `-w` | Specifies the wordlist |
| `-t 10` | Sets the number of concurrent threads |
| `-o results.txt` | Writes results to an output file |
| `-x .php,.txt,.js` | Tests additional file extensions |
| `-r` | Follows redirects |
| `-k` | Skips TLS certificate verification; use only when appropriate in an authorized lab |

Option availability and behavior can vary by Gobuster version. Consult the installed version's help output:

```bash
gobuster dir --help
```

#### Common HTTP Status Codes

| Code | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `200 OK` | The request succeeded |
| `301 Moved Permanently` | The resource has a permanent redirect |
| `302 Found` | A temporary redirect is indicated |
| `403 Forbidden` | The server refuses the request |
| `404 Not Found` | The requested resource was not found |

Interpret status codes in context. For example, a `403` response can indicate that a path exists but access is denied; it does not prove that the path contains sensitive information.

Potentially interesting paths might include:

- `development.log`: Could expose debugging or application information if improperly accessible.
- `/private/`: The name alone does not establish whether access is restricted.
- `/robots.txt`: May contain crawler instructions and path clues.
- `/sitemap.xml`: May list discoverable application URLs.

**Discovery is not the same as exploitation.** Validate each finding before drawing a security conclusion.

